  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />
  <h1>What Is E-Waste?</h1>
  <p class="lede">E-waste (electronic waste) refers to any discarded electrical or electronic equipment that is no longer useful, including computers, mobile phones, home appliances, and medical devices. In India, e-waste is regulated under the E-Waste (Management) Rules 2022, and unauthorized disposal carries environmental and legal risks.</p>

  <CtaBar whatsappMessage="Hi, I have a question about What Is E-Waste?." />

  <ul class="key-takeaways">
    <li>E-waste covers any end-of-life electrical or electronic device that relies on a battery or power source.</li>
    <li>Household e-waste includes laptops, smartphones, televisions, refrigerators, and washing machines.</li>
    <li>Improper disposal releases toxic substances into soil and water, posing risks to human health and the environment.</li>
  </ul>

  <section>
    <h2>Definition</h2>
    <p>E-waste covers any end-of-life electrical or electronic device that relies on a battery or power source. It spans consumer electronics, IT equipment, medical devices, and automatic dispensers — all of which may contain hazardous substances like lead, mercury, cadmium, and brominated flame retardants.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Common Categories</h2>
    <p>Household e-waste includes laptops, smartphones, televisions, refrigerators, and washing machines. Commercial e-waste includes servers, networking gear, printers, and office phones. Medical and industrial e-waste covers diagnostic equipment, lab instruments, and manufacturing controllers.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Why Proper Disposal Matters</h2>
    <p>Improper disposal releases toxic substances into soil and water, posing risks to human health and the environment. Authorized recyclers like Ewaste Kochi process e-waste through scientifically designed facilities, recovering metals and neutralizing hazardous fractions under CPCB supervision.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details>
  <summary>Is old mobile phone e-waste?</summary>
  <p>Yes, end-of-life smartphones and tablets are classified as e-waste under Indian regulations.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>Can I throw e-waste in regular trash?</summary>
  <p>No. Regular trash disposal of e-waste is prohibited under E-Waste Rules 2022 and can result in penalties.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>What happens to recycled e-waste?</summary>
  <p>Authorized recyclers dismantle devices, recover metals, and safely treat hazardous fractions — issuing a Certificate of Recycling.</p>
</details>
  </section>

